Long COVID Dysautonomia Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-viral orthostatic intolerance

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-024
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe syncopal risk, sudden postural tachycardia, and inability to maintain upright posture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe dizziness upon standing, profound fatigue, and an episode of near-collapse while walking to the bathroom. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out cardiogenic syncope, severe volume depletion, and to evaluate orthostatic hemodynamics to prevent traumatic falls prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: If I sit up my heart starts racing out of my chest, my vision goes black, and my legs give way.
History of Present Illness: Patient with a post-viral syndrome reports a severe 3-day exacerbation of dysautonomia. The patient experiences a dramatic tachycardia upon standing, accompanied by severe pre-syncope (tunnel vision, dizziness) and profound fatigue. The timeline indicates progressive worsening, where the patient is now totally unable to tolerate upright posture for more than 30 seconds without feeling they will pass out.
Functional Status: Bedbound tonight; requires physical assistance for all transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fall risk; highly reliant on parent for hydration and safety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 supine, 110/75 standing mmHg | HR: 72 supine, 128 standing bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Pale when upright, anxious, immediately seeking to lie down.
Physical Exam: Postural vital signs reveal an archetypal post-viral orthostatic intolerance (POTS-pattern) with an excessive heart rate increase (+56 bpm) upon standing without significant blood pressure drop. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates regular rhythm without murmurs. Neurological exam is globally intact. Mucous membranes are moist, excluding severe clinical dehydration.
Environmental Observations: Dim lighting, clear paths to bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular chronotropic or hypotensive agents being taken.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Long COVID Dysautonomia Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cardiogenic Syncope (Arrhythmia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia is strictly tied to postural changes rather than occurring randomly at rest, and auscultation reveals regular rhythm.

Severe Hypovolemia / Dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of significant orthostatic hypotension (blood pressure maintained) and clinically moist mucous membranes.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal oxygen saturations, clear chest, and heart rate returns to normal immediately upon lying flat.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute inability to stand without severe tachycardia and pre-syncope posed an imminent risk of traumatic injury and required urgent exclusion of life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias. By confirming a purely orthostatic tachycardia without hypotension, the diagnosis of post-viral dysautonomia was confirmed in the home. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in an unnecessary ED presentation for suspected cardiac event. Immediate implementation of aggressive counter-maneuvers and fluid/sodium loading was required to restore functional mobility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral rehydration salts and immediate increase in dietary sodium to artificially expand blood volume. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Naltrexone (LDN), Low Dose Tadalafil, and NAD+.
Positive Behavioral Change: By drinking 500ml of cold electrolyte fluid exactly 15 minutes before getting out of bed, you will acutely constrict your blood vessels and expand your blood volume, directly preventing your heart rate from spiking and rewarding you with safe standing.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Shower chair mandated for all bathing.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to consider formal autonomic testing and initiation of beta-blockers or Ivabradine.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient experiences actual loss of consciousness (fainting), if chest pain develops, or if the heart rate remains above 120 bpm while lying completely flat.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The autonomic nervous system is severely dysregulated following the viral infection; recovery is non-linear and requires aggressive lifestyle modification.
Clinical Course: Postural symptoms will improve with aggressive fluid and salt loading, but true resolution requires targeted medications and graded autonomic rehabilitation over months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you actually pass out and hit the floor, or if your heart continues to race wildly even after lying down for 20 minutes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your blood vessels are failing to constrict when you stand, causing blood to pool in your legs; your heart is racing rapidly to try and pump blood back up to your brain.
Actionable Steps: Perform leg muscle clenching (pumping your calves) before standing; this mechanically pushes blood back to your heart, actively preventing the dizzy spells and reinforcing safer mobility.

